/* book Worked example 2: Q: An alternator regulates to 14.2 V measured at its own B+ terminal. The charging cable from B+ to the battery is 1.2 m of 10 mm² copper (ρ = 0.0172 Ω·mm²/m), and a corroded joint on it adds 0.004 Ω. The alternator delivers 80 A, of which the loads near the alternator take none. (a) Find the cable resistance and the voltage the battery receives. (b) Repeat with the corroded joint cleaned. (c) Explain why a separate sense wire to the battery fixes the problem.
   1. Cable resistance: R = ρl ÷ A = 0.0172 × 1.2 ÷ 10 = 0.00206 Ω.
   2. With the corroded joint: R = 0.00206 + 0.004 = 0.00606 Ω; drop = 80 A × 0.00606 Ω = 0.485 V; battery voltage = 14.2 − 0.485 = 13.7 V.
   3. Cleaned joint: drop = 80 A × 0.00206 Ω = 0.165 V; battery voltage = 14.2 − 0.165 = 14.0 V.
   4. At 13.7 V a flooded battery charges slowly and may never reach full charge on short trips.
   5. A sense wire carries almost no current, so it has almost no drop: it reports the true battery voltage. The regulator then raises B+ until the battery sees 14.2 V, which here means about 14.7 V at B+ with the bad joint.
   Answer: The cable is 0.00206 Ω; with the bad joint the battery gets only 13.7 V (0.485 V lost), with a clean joint 14.0 V. Battery sensing makes the regulator compensate the drop, but the corroded joint should still be cleaned. */
