/* book exam traps: The regulator controls field current, not engine speed. Revving the engine does not raise the regulated bus voltage above its set point. | A dead charge warning lamp can stop charging because it supplies initial field current on many machines. | Do not use open-circuit battery voltage as the charging voltage. A 12 V battery at rest is about 12.4 V; the bus runs near 14 V. | The B− path is not just the thin wire. Engine block and chassis grounds carry the return current; a bad ground causes wrong sensing and dim lamps. | Higher temperature usually calls for a lower charge voltage, not higher. */
