/* book Worked example 2: Q: An alternator has a zero-ampere speed n₀ = 1000 rpm and a rated current of 120 A at 6000 rpm. Use the self-limiting model I = Imax(1 − n₀ ÷ n). The pulley ratio is 2.5 and the engine idles at 800 rpm. The loads at night are 70 A. (a) Find Imax. (b) Find the alternator current at idle and say whether the battery charges or discharges. (c) Find the engine speed at which the alternator covers the 70 A.
   1. At rated speed: 120 = Imax(1 − 1000 ÷ 6000) = Imax × 0.8333, so Imax = 120 ÷ 0.8333 = 144 A.
   2. Alternator speed at idle: Nalt = 800 × 2.5 = 2000 rpm.
   3. Current at idle: I = 144 × (1 − 1000 ÷ 2000) = 144 × 0.5 = 72 A.
   4. 72 A is more than the 70 A load, so 2 A is left for the battery: it charges, but only just.
   5. For exactly 70 A: 70 = 144(1 − 1000 ÷ n), so 1 − 1000 ÷ n = 0.4861, 1000 ÷ n = 0.5139 and n = 1946 rpm at the alternator.
   6. Engine speed = 1946 ÷ 2.5 = 778 rpm. Below this engine speed the battery must help.
   Answer: Imax is 144 A; at an 800 rpm idle the alternator turns at 2000 rpm and gives 72 A, so the battery gains only 2 A; the loads are covered down to an engine speed of about 778 rpm. */
